From d3776d0f43b80634426016126bc5cd7ad7c2b786 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 07:25:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 63/69] Find recursive functions with Tarjan's algorithm

MarkRecursions marked a function recursive by searching, from each
function, every simple path through the call graph back to it. The
search clears its on-path flag on the way back, so it explores the
paths through a function again each time it reaches it: time
exponential in the number of cycles. A whole-program compile of
NetHack, whose direct calls form one cycle of 279 functions (through
panic() and done()), was still in MarkCycle when sampled after one and
four minutes.

A function lies on a cycle exactly when it shares a strongly connected
component with another function or calls itself, so mark those, found
by an iterative Tarjan search in one walk of the graph. A temporary
check that ran both searches found the same functions recursive in
each of 308 programs: the autotest sources, nethack64's compiler
repros and its 108 NetHack measurement compiles.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
